@mathbraga Thanks for the review of this. It took me a while to understand the original code (which is partly why I rewrote it to try and make it clearer, and marginally more performant).

`hasOverlappingCommits` should return true when two commits that are being connected have other commits between them on the source branch (i.e, the same branch as `commitA` in my rewrite). This being the case, the arrow's path then needs to be diverted so as to avoid running over the other commits and making the graph unclear.

Hope this makes sense but do keep asking questions if you have any, as it may be that I've missed something.

I see. I don't know which approach is the right one, I would simply do:
```
const shouldRerouteArrow = (commitA, commitB, allCommits) => {
  const p1 = commitPos[commitA.id];
  const p2 = commitPos[commitB.id];
  const commitBIsFurthest = dir === 'TB' ? p1.x < p2.x : p1.y < p2.y;
  ...
```
simply because of arity reduction.
